{"explanation":"Great follow-up! When the array is already sorted and we always pick the first element as pivot, every partition gives us one empty side. We get N partitions of size N, N-1, N-2... leading to O(n²) — as bad as Bubble Sort! The fix: pick a random pivot or use median-of-three.","mood":"thinking","code":{"language":"python","snippet":"import random

def quick_sort_random(arr, low, high):
    if low < high:
        # Random pivot avoids worst-case on sorted arrays
        rand_idx = random.randint(low, high)
        arr[rand_idx], arr[high] = arr[high], arr[rand_idx]
        pivot_idx = partition(arr, low, high)
        quick_sort_random(arr, low, pivot_idx - 1)
        quick_sort_random(arr, pivot_idx + 1, high)"},"visualSequence":{"type":"sorting","title":"QuickSort Worst Case: Sorted Array","steps":[{"step":1,"action":"highlight","description":"First element selected as pivot (bad choice!)","elements":[0]},{"step":2,"action":"compare","description":"All other elements are larger — right partition only","elements":[0,1]},{"step":3,"action":"compare","description":"Next pivot again picks smallest — unbalanced again","elements":[1,2]},{"step":4,"action":"highlight","description":"N levels deep — O(n²) total work","elements":[2]}]},"suggestedFollowUps":["What is the median-of-three pivot strategy?","How does MergeSort guarantee O(n log n) regardless of input?"]}

{"explanation":"Imagine exploring a maze. BFS uses a queue — it checks all rooms on level 1 before going to level 2, like ripples in water. DFS uses a stack — it dives deep down one path until it hits a dead end, then backtracks. BFS finds shortest paths; DFS uses less memory on wide graphs.","mood":"explaining","code":{"language":"python","snippet":"from collections import deque

def bfs(graph, start):
    visited, queue = set(), deque([start])
    while queue:
        node = queue.popleft()  # FIFO — level by level
        if node not in visited:
            visited.add(node)
            queue.extend(graph[node])
    return visited

def dfs(graph, start, visited=None):
    if visited is None: visited = set()
    visited.add(start)
    for neighbor in graph[start]:  # LIFO via recursion stack
        if neighbor not in visited:
            dfs(graph, neighbor, visited)
    return visited"},"visualSequence":{"type":"graph","title":"BFS vs DFS Graph Traversal","steps":[{"step":1,"action":"visit","description":"Start at node A","elements":[0]},{"step":2,"action":"traverse","description":"BFS: Visit all neighbors of A (level 1)","elements":[1,2,3]},{"step":3,"action":"traverse","description":"BFS: Visit all neighbors of level 1 nodes","elements":[4,5,6]},{"step":4,"action":"visit","description":"DFS: Go deep — A → B → D → H","elements":[0,1,3,7]},{"step":5,"action":"traverse","description":"DFS: Backtrack when dead end reached","elements":[7,3]}]},"suggestedFollowUps":["When would you choose BFS over DFS for a real problem?","How does Dijkstra's algorithm extend BFS for weighted graphs?"]}

{"explanation":"Fibonacci without memoization recalculates fib(3) dozens of times — it's like forgetting your homework and redoing it each class! DP says: write it down once. We store each result in a table. fib(n) = fib(n-1) + fib(n-2), but we only compute each subproblem once — O(n) time, O(n) space.","mood":"explaining","code":{"language":"python","snippet":"def fib_dp(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return n
    # Build table bottom-up
    dp = [0] * (n + 1)
    dp[1] = 1
    for i in range(2, n + 1):
        dp[i] = dp[i-1] + dp[i-2]  # use stored results
    return dp[n]

# Space-optimized: only need last two values
def fib_optimized(n):
    a, b = 0, 1
    for _ in range(n):
        a, b = b, a + b
    return a"},"visualSequence":{"type":"dp","title":"Fibonacci DP Table Build","steps":[{"step":1,"action":"highlight","description":"Base cases: dp[0]=0, dp[1]=1","elements":[0,1]},{"step":2,"action":"compare","description":"dp[2] = dp[1] + dp[0] = 1","elements":[1,2]},{"step":3,"action":"compare","description":"dp[3] = dp[2] + dp[1] = 2","elements":[2,3]},{"step":4,"action":"compare","description":"dp[4] = dp[3] + dp[2] = 3","elements":[3,4]},{"step":5,"action":"highlight","description":"Each value computed exactly once — O(n)","elements":[4]}]},"suggestedFollowUps":["What is the difference between top-down memoization and bottom-up tabulation?","Can you show me the 0/1 Knapsack problem using DP?"]}
